    "synthesis": {
        "synthesis_style": "tutorial",
        "comparative_synthesis_degree": "very high",
        "lesson_blocks": [
            {
                "location": "Section IV.A.2 (DSRC Discussion)",
                "lesson": "DSRC suffers from Doppler effects at high speeds (>160km/h), NLOS significantly reduces range (~50%), and congestion causes severe QoS degradation due to EDCA MAC protocol. TDMA or centralized control may address congestion issues.",
                "locator": "L547-622"
            },
            {
                "location": "Section IV.B.2 (C-V2X Discussion)",
                "lesson": "C-V2X has more robust physical layer but suffers from centralized core latency (4G LTE). 5G NR with distributed 5GC addresses latency. SB-SPS mechanism has half-duplex collision issues. Flexible numerology helps with TTI-related latency.",
                "locator": "L720-750"
            },
            {
                "location": "Section IV.C.3 (Summary)",
                "lesson": "C-V2X has superior application coverage and graceful degradation under congestion. DSRC has latency advantages in uncongested close-range scenarios. 802.11bd constrained by backward compatibility; 5G NR designed for evolution. C-V2X is the more suitable candidate.",
                "locator": "L803-810"
            }
        ],
        "aha_moment": "The paper's key insight is that the DSRC vs C-V2X debate should not be framed as 'which is better' but 'which has smaller gap to close'. While DSRC excels in uncongested direct communications, C-V2X already covers all V2X types (V2V/V2I/V2N/V2P) through existing infrastructure. The remaining challenges for C-V2X (sidelink congestion, latency) are being actively addressed by 5G NR evolution, whereas DSRC would need to build entirely new infrastructure for V2N/V2P coverage."
    },
